--TEST--
A writer writes one document type declaration above the root element, and refuses a second one and a late one without writing a byte — `rule:core-classes/xml-tree-and-stream`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Xml\Writer::doctype
// A prolog carries at most one declaration, so a second call would leave a
// document no parser accepts. A comment before it and one between it and
// the root are both allowed.
Core\Xml\Writer $w = Core\Xml::writer();
$w->startDocument();
$w->comment("first");
$w->doctype("html");
try {
    $w->doctype("html");
} catch (LogicError $twice) {
    echo $twice->message, "\n";
}
try {
    $w->doctype("other");
} catch (LogicError $other) {
    echo $other->message, "\n";
}
$w->comment("second");
$w->startElement("html");
$w->endElement();
try {
    $w->doctype("html");
} catch (LogicError $late) {
    echo $late->message, "\n";
}
echo $w->endDocument(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Xml\Writer::doctype(): a document has at most one document type declaration, and one is already written.
Core\Xml\Writer::doctype(): a document has at most one document type declaration, and one is already written.
Core\Xml\Writer::doctype(): a document type declaration goes above the root element, and the root element is already written.
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><!--first--><!DOCTYPE html><!--second--><html/>
